      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the sweet pork. Fry the pork cubes in a dry frying pan over medium heat for 8 minutes until browned and the fat has rendered. Add the {{palm-sugar}}, {{soy-sauce-dark}}, and {{fish-sauce}}. Stir and cook for 4 to 5 minutes until the pork is coated in a dark, sticky glaze. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fry the shallots in 2 tablespoons of oil over medium heat until golden and crispy. Remove and set aside on kitchen paper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat 1 tablespoon of oil in a wok over high heat. Add the {{shrimp-paste}} and fry, stirring, for 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the cold rice. Toss and fry for 4 minutes over high heat until the rice is hot and coated with the paste."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve the rice on plates. Top with the sweet pork, crispy shallots, egg strips or a fried egg, and fresh coriander. Add lime wedges on the side."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Rice smells overpoweringly of shrimp paste",
              "cause": "Shrimp paste not fried long enough before rice was added",
              "fix": "Fry the paste for a full minute, stirring constantly, until the raw smell softens to a savoury one."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Sweet pork glaze has burnt",
              "cause": "Too much palm sugar or heat too high",
              "fix": "Lower the heat as soon as the sugar goes in. Stir constantly and remove from heat the moment it looks glossy and caramelised."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Shallots are soft rather than crispy",
              "cause": "Not fried at high enough temperature or added to damp oil",
              "fix": "Make sure the oil is properly hot before adding sliced shallots. Fry in a thin layer and move constantly."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Khao kluk kapi is a more complex dish than most Thai fried rice recipes. It has royal origins and the multiple components reflect a more elaborate style of cooking. Shrimp paste fried rice is the foundation, but the sweet pork, crispy shallots, and dressed garnishes are what make it distinctive. The dish is a study in contrasting textures and tastes: salty and sweet pork, pungent rice, sharp lime, crispy shallots. Every component is eaten together, not separately."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
